### Section 1: Symbol Tables — Functional vs Imperative (and insertion)

From slides 4-7, 10-17, 23:
- Symbol table = environment: set of bindings identifier ↦ (type/location). σ = {g ↦ string, a ↦ int}.
- Declarations bind; uses look up.
- Extending: σ1 = σ0 + {a ↦ int, ...}; bindings on right override left; "+" is not commutative (X + Y ≠ Y + X). Lookup in σ + {x ↦ τ} returns newest x.
- Lexical scopes: at end of scope, local bindings discarded (σ3 → σ1 at line 6, σ1 → σ0 at line 8).
- API: add / lookup / undo (S_empty, S_enter, S_look, S_beginScope, S_endScope).

Functional style:
- σ' = σ + {a ↦ τ} without mutating σ; σ stays available for lookup (pristine); previous environments remain valid; restoring = just discard the new table / go back to the old pointer. Multiple environments can be active at once.
- Implementation: persistent/immutable structures. With hash tables: not efficient — adding a binding in a bucket would modify the shared array; copying the array is O(size) ("The array in a hash table should be quite large" → copy is expensive).
- Efficient functional: balanced binary search tree; adding a node at depth d creates d new nodes (path copy, "path copying"), no full copy; lookup O(log n) for balanced tree; m1 unchanged, m2 = m1 + {mouse ↦ 4}.
- Multiple symbol tables: per-class/module tables combined: σ7 = σ2 + σ4 + σ6 (Java compiles E,N,D all in σ7 due to forward references; ML compiles N in σ0+σ2, D in σ0+σ2+σ4, result {M ↦ σ7}).

Imperative style:
- modify σ1 until it becomes σ2; while σ2 exists, cannot look up in σ1; when done with σ2, undo the modification to get σ1 back.
- Implementation: single global environment σ + an undo stack (auxiliary stack); destructive update; hash table with external chaining — efficient lookup; deletion handled via chaining.
- Insertion behavior (key difference!): new binding a ↦ τ2 inserted at the HEAD of the bucket chain, keeping the older a ↦ τ1 in the chain: hash(a) -> <a,τ2> -> <a,τ1>. Lookup finds the head (newest) first → shadowing (scope-compliant). pop removes the head binding → restores a ↦ τ1. Insert/pop work stack-like (LIFO), so scopes can be ended and the table rewinds.
- Restore mechanics: undo stack (auxiliary stack) records order symbols were pushed; each popped symbol removes head binding of its bucket; beginScope pushes a special marker; endScope pops until marker. Integrated into Binder via prevtop and a global `top` pointer: pushing copies top into prevtop of the new binder.

Insertion operations difference (the explicit question):
- Functional: insertion = compute a NEW table σ' (persistent); original σ unchanged. Underlying technique: path/copy of only the modified path (BST: d new nodes; hash: would require array copy — inefficient). Old and new environments coexist; "undo" = forget new table (O(1) pointer). Order of environments: you can look up in the old environment while the new exists. Stack-like: newest binding wins in lookup; to "undo" you just use the older table.
- Imperative: insertion = destructive update of the single global table; new binding is pushed at head of bucket (shadowing older binding); original state not directly available while new exists — only recoverable by undo: pop the head (LIFO). The undo stack makes it effectively a stack; endScope pops until marker. So insertion must be reversible; hence pushing at head rather than overwriting (overwriting would lose the old binding and can't undo).

That's the key contrast: functional insertion preserves the old table so no undo log needed; imperative insertion must be undoable → shadow via chain head + undo stack, restoring by popping.

