## 1. Functional vs imperative symbol tables

### 1.1 Common picture: environments and `+` (slides 4–6)
(Run through the running example f: σ0 → σ1 → σ2 → σ3 and discard.)
- right-hand bindings override; `X + Y` ≠ `Y + X`; lookup finds the newest binding.
- scopes: when the analyzer reaches the end of a scope, local bindings are discarded (line 6: σ3 discarded, back to σ1; line 8: σ1 discarded, back to σ0).
- symbol table must support: add / lookup / undo bindings.

### 1.2 Functional style (slides 7, 14–17)
- Definition: compute σ′ = σ + {a ↦ τ} without changing σ. σ stays "pristine"; you can still look up identifiers in the old environment; restoring is trivial: just go back to the old table.
- Each addition produces a NEW table; the environment grows into a persistent tree of versions...

