### 1.3 Imperative style (slides 7, 10–13, 23–26)
- Definition: modify σ1 until it becomes σ2; while σ2 is alive, σ1 is not available; when σ2 is no longer needed, undo the modifications to get σ1 back. Recipe: one single global environment σ + an "undo stack".
- Efficient implementation: hash table (fast lookup); deletion needed for scope exit → hash table with external chaining (bucket lists); insert-only-at-head discipline + undo stack.
- In the Tiger compiler's C implementation: destructive-update environments; S_beginScope/S_endScope implemented over a generic TAB module; the undo stack is folded into the bucket nodes via a prevtop field.

### 1.4 The insertion operations compared (the important difference)
Table: aspect | functional | imperative
And bullet detail on the "a ↦ τ2 over a ↦ τ1" case (slides 12–13):
- functional: σ′ = σ + {a ↦ τ2} is a new structure; σ still contains a ↦ τ1; nothing is modified; no undo needed.
- imperative: insert leaves a ↦ τ1 in the bucket and puts a ↦ τ2 earlier in the list — hash(a) -> <a, τ2> -> <a, τ1>; lookup returns the newest (shadowing ✓). At scope exit pop(a) removes the head → hash(a) -> <a, τ1> — σ restored; insert/pop behave stack-like (LIFO).

Then a short "why" paragraph: Because the imperative table is shared and mutated, each insertion must be reversible; the only way to undo "insert τ2" is to keep τ1 around underneath → hence never overwrite, push at head, log the order of pushes (undo stack / prevtop). In the functional world nothing is destroyed, so there is nothing to undo — the old table itself is the "undo".

