### Listing 7 — C: the implementation of symbols (slide 22)
```c
static S_symbol mksymbol (string name , S_symbol next) {
  S_symbol s = checked_malloc(sizeof(*s));
  s->name = name; s->next = next;
  return s;
}

S_symbol S_symbol (string name) {
	int index = hash(name)%SIZE;
	S_symbol syms = hashtable[index], sym;
	for ( sym = syms; sym; sym = sym->next)
	  if (0 == strcmp(sym->name, name)) return sym;
	sym = mksymbol(name,syms);
	hashtable[index] = sym;
   return sym;
}

string S_name (S_symbol sym) {
  return sym->name;
}
```
Explanation:
- mksymbol allocates one symbol node (name + chain link).
- S_symbol(name): interning — if `name` is already in the global symbol hash table, return the existing unique symbol; otherwise create it and push it into the bucket. So equal strings always map to the same S_symbol object.
- S_name(sym): the original string (for printing).
- why (slide 20): "In Program 5.2: must examine every character of the string s for the hash operation; each time it compares s against a string in the ith bucket. How to improve? Convert each string to a symbol." The Symbol module properties: 
  - use the Symbol-pointer itself as the integer hash-key → very fast hash; 
  - equality = pointer/integer comparison (fast); 
  - "greater-than" comparison in arbitrary order also fast → good for BSTs (the functional tables!). 
