### Listing 9 — C: beginScope / endScope (slide 24)
```c
static struct S_symbol_ marksym = { “<mark>”, 0 };

void S_beginScope ( S_table t) { 
  S_enter(t, &marksym, NULL); 
}

void S_endScope( S_table t) {
  S_symbol s;
  do 
    s= TAB_pop(t); 
  while (s != &marksym);
}
```
Explanation:
- slide text: "For destructive-update environments: S_beginScope remembers the current state of the table; S_endScope restores the table to where it was at the most recent beginScope that has not already been ended."
- begin attaches a distinguished marker binding ("<mark>") into the table; end pops bindings (in LIFO order, using the auxiliary stack / prevtop chain) until it pops the marker → exactly undoes everything inserted since beginScope.
- this is the imperative "restore" mechanism = undo to the remembered state.

### Listing 10 — C: TAB_table_ and Binder (slide 26)
```c
struct TAB_table_ {
  binder table[TABSIZE];
  void *top;
};

t->table[index] = Binder(key, value,t->table[index], t->top);

static binder Binder(void *key, void *value, binder next, void *prevtop) {
  binder b = checked_malloc(sizeof(*b));
  b->key = key; b->value=value; b->next=next; 
  b->prevtop = prevtop;
  return b;
}
```
Explanation:
- slide text: "The auxiliary stack can be integrated into the Binder by having a global variable top showing the most recent Symbol bound in the table. Pushing: copy top into the prevtop field of the Binder."
- two links per node: `next` = external chain within a bucket; `prevtop` = the undo stack link (the previously most-recent binder). `top` = top of the undo stack.
- Binder(...) creates the node and records prevtop; the insert line pushes it: table[index] = new node whose next is the old chain head and whose prevtop is the old top; then top would be updated to the new node.
- pop: remove the node from its chain and set top = prevtop → restores the previous state (scope-style undo, O(1) per pop).
- This is the concrete "single global environment + undo stack" from slide 7.

